<DocumentLayout
  title="Local installation"
  description="How to test a local Debian build of Locus before the first public release."
>
  <h1>Try a local build.</h1>
  <p>
    Locus does not have a public installer yet. The current packaging work is for testing
    Debian-based Linux systems locally.
  </p>
  <h2>If you already have a Debian package</h2>
  <p>
    Open the folder containing the <code>.deb</code> file. You can install it with your system’s
    software installer, or use:
  </p>
  <pre><code>sudo apt install ./Locus_0.1.0_amd64.deb</code></pre>
  <p>
    Use the actual filename of your package. Once installed, open <strong>Locus</strong> from your
    application launcher. A packaged build does not need Node.js or a development server to run.
  </p>
  <h2>Building from source</h2>
  <p>
    This step currently needs development tools. The repository’s
    <a href="https://github.com/HYP3R00T/Locus/blob/main/docs/releasing.md"
      >build and release guide</a
    >
    lists the setup requirements and local build commands.
  </p>
  <pre><code>npm ci
mise run build</code></pre>
  <p>
    Packages are written to <code>packages/src-tauri/target/release/bundle/deb/</code>. A local
    build is not a public release and is not uploaded automatically.
  </p>
  <h2>Other platforms</h2>
  <p>
    Debian is the current packaging focus. Windows installers, AppImage, and Flatpak are possible
    distribution options, but are not offered here as tested downloads.
  </p>
  <h2>Before relying on a development build</h2>
  <p>
    Keep backups of important drawings and study data. Try reading, drawing, closing, and reopening
    the app before using a build for an extended study session.
  </p>
</DocumentLayout>
